## 2008 debate over credit-card “rate-jacking”

In 2008, consumer groups and lawmakers intensified criticism of “rate-jacking”—raising a cardholder’s interest rate, often on balances already incurred, even when the customer had paid the card issuer on time. Banks defended the practice as necessary risk-based pricing during a worsening credit crisis; critics argued that it amounted to changing the price of a loan after the money had been borrowed.

### Citi’s rate increases and justification

Late in 2008, Citigroup notified some cardholders that their annual percentage rates would rise, reportedly by about three percentage points on average. The move drew particular criticism because Citi had earlier promised to curb “any time, any reason” repricing.

Citi said its pledge permitted increases when economic or market conditions changed. It cited:

- the rapidly deteriorating economy and consumer-credit environment;
- rising delinquencies and expected loan losses;
- higher funding costs; and
- the need to price accounts according to their increased risk.

Thus, Citi maintained that the increases were not arbitrary punishment of individual customers, but a portfolio-wide response to extraordinary market conditions.

### Customers’ opt-out option

Affected Citi customers could reject the new rate. Generally, opting out meant that:

- the old rate continued to apply while the balance was repaid;
- the customer could keep using the card under the existing terms for a limited period—often until the card’s expiration; and
- the account would then be closed or would no longer be renewed.

Consumer advocates said this was only a limited choice: customers could avoid the increase, but at the cost of losing access to the credit line, potentially harming their credit utilization and credit score.

### Credit Cardholders’ Bill of Rights

The House responded with the **Credit Cardholders’ Bill of Rights Act of 2008**, sponsored by Representative Carolyn Maloney. The House passed it in September 2008 with bipartisan support.

Among other protections, the measure would have:

- sharply restricted retroactive interest-rate increases on existing balances;
- required advance notice of significant changes in account terms;
- prohibited or limited “double-cycle billing”;
- required payments above the minimum to be allocated more fairly, generally toward higher-rate balances;
- prevented issuers from treating a payment as late when it was received by the stated due date and time; and
- given consumers a clearer opportunity to reject changed terms.

The banking industry warned that these limits would make credit less available or more expensive for all cardholders. Supporters countered that lenders should set prices before extending credit rather than raising rates after consumers had incurred balances.

### Christopher Dodd’s Senate bill

Senate Banking Committee Chairman **Christopher Dodd** introduced a Senate credit-card reform bill, commonly called the **Credit Card Accountability, Responsibility and Disclosure Act**. It addressed many of the same concerns as the House bill and sought to:

- prevent most rate increases from being applied to existing balances;
- end “universal default,” in which an issuer raised a rate because of a problem with an unrelated creditor;
- require substantial advance notice of rate and fee increases;
- improve payment allocation and billing practices;
- restrict penalty fees and other practices regarded as unfair; and
- strengthen protections for young consumers.

Dodd argued that card contracts had become one-sided and that issuers should not be able to rewrite the cost of previously extended credit. His 2008 proposal did not become law that year, but it helped form the basis of the **Credit CARD Act of 2009**.

### Pending Federal Reserve rules

At the same time, the Federal Reserve and other banking regulators were considering rules under their authority to prohibit unfair or deceptive practices. The proposed rules would have:

- barred most interest-rate increases on existing balances, subject to limited exceptions such as expiration of a disclosed promotional rate, changes in an indexed variable rate, or serious delinquency;
- required advance notice—generally 45 days—of important account changes;
- restricted double-cycle billing;
- required more equitable allocation of payments; and
- given customers a reasonable amount of time to receive and pay their bills.

The Federal Reserve finalized major portions of these reforms in December 2008, although most were scheduled to take effect later. Congress subsequently enacted broader statutory protections in the 2009 CARD Act.

Overall, the dispute centered on whether issuers needed flexibility to reprice risk during a financial crisis or whether raising rates on existing debt was inherently unfair. Citi’s opt-out offered some protection, but the House bill, Dodd proposal, and Federal Reserve rules all moved toward a stronger principle: new rates generally should apply to new borrowing, not retroactively to balances consumers had already accumulated.
